  "statement": "The Safetyist wants to save us by building a wall, and the Skeptic fears the wall is already being used to lock them out. Both are right about the danger, but wrong about the cure. We do not need a regulatory gatekeeper to protect us from systemic failure. We need to treat safety as a competitive engineering challenge where real-world telemetry, not pre-deployment paperwork, dictates who survives in the market. The automotive industry proved this; seatbelts and anti-lock brakes became standard because companies feared the massive tort liability of shipping death traps, not because a central bureaucrat mandated them in 1950. When we force developers to pay for their own failures through strict, output-based liability, we create a race to the top that no committee can replicate.\n\nTiered liability regimes are currently being designed as regulatory moats to insulate incumbents from the disruption of open-source progress. If we allow a system where only firms with 500-person compliance departments can clear certification hurdles, we guarantee that innovation stops at the boardroom door of a few massive labs. We should look at the National Transportation Safety Board as the only viable model for governance. They do not issue pre-flight licenses for every engine design; they investigate failures after they happen, derive systemic lessons, and force the entire industry to adapt or face bankruptcy. This separation of halt authority from licensing authority prevents the very capture that the Skeptic rightly fears while keeping the pipeline of innovation open for the next generation of builders.\n\nEvery day we delay the deployment of an AI-driven medical diagnostic tool because of a static, 18-month certification queue is a day we choose to let thousands of patients suffer from preventable, treatable conditions. This isn't caution; it is a passive moral atrocity that inflicts a tangible human toll on the vulnerable. We must implement a framework of continuous, automated telemetry that flags output anomalies in real-time. This shifts the burden from speculative, whiteboard-based fear to empirical, data-driven accountability. If your model fails, the liability costs should be high enough to bankrupt your firm, not just trigger a fine. Who gave the regulators the right to trade away human lives for the comfort of a 'safe' but stagnant status quo?",
